[AS7's Problem 11.75 (a and b, the power system)]

e,1,0,240
r1,1,0,80-50j
r2,1,0,120+70j
r3,1,0,60

analysis: ac
omega: omega
note: pf with ve and -ie reads 0.99805 leading, which is what makes part (c) come out the way it does.
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
evaluate: -se


[AS7's Problem 11.75 (c, trying a capacitor)]

e,1,0,240
r1,1,0,80-50j
r2,1,0,120+70j
r3,1,0,60
c,1,0,x

analysis: ac
omega: 2*pi*50
note: No positive capacitance can work: part (b) showed the load is already leading, so a capacitor pushes it further the wrong way. Probably why the textbook gives no number here.
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
solve_equations: im(se) = 0
solve_unknowns: x
solve_real_only: yes


[AS7's Problem 11.75 (c, an inductor instead)]

e,1,0,240
r1,1,0,80-50j
r2,1,0,120+70j
r3,1,0,60
l,1,0,x

analysis: ac
omega: 2*pi*50
note: Positive, and putting it back in as the inductor's value brings the power factor to 1.
rounding: 5
si: no
units: yes
rms: yes
polar: no
solve_equations: im(se) = 0
solve_unknowns: x
solve_real_only: yes
